The approved M0 record attributes its backbone to Yperman (2007) and UNsim v0.12.0, commit 5c396357cc540f2433c3e33689b415b1df1f6e79. This consolidation checks local approved equations/code; it does not newly reproduce those full original sources. Neither Gong nor another paper is credited with the whole new urban scan/access operator.

## Native rival and evidence limits

[Native-rival source record](urban_aggregate_rival_source_notes.md)

[Closed selection policy](../../temp_content/research_readiness/aggregate_rival_questions.md)

The B1 working source remains [Wei et al., arXiv v2](https://arxiv.org/html/2310.12249v2), section 2. The B2 working direction retains [Gong et al.](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0191261525000578) with a sourced storage/access adaptation, for which [Liu–Chang (2011)](https://attap.umd.edu/wp-content/uploads/2022/05/TRC522.pdf) remains a candidate.

These are working sources, not qualified current implementation instances. No full native algorithm reproduction or theorem transfer to a new bay-entry composition is claimed. The source-specific causal, storage/signal and notation qualifications in the existing notes still apply. Feedback 8 permits implementation-dependent instantiation later. Strong legal macro inference remains eligible.

The earlier exclusive-target mixture ablation remains unselected and archived. Changing the representative permissions does not qualify or resurrect that comparator: the new topology also has parallel through access. The agreed native baseline policy remains; no weaker benchmark is introduced.

The current checks are exact local arithmetic and definition-level reasoning. The previous sixteen hand checks belong to the earlier consolidated report and are not rerun or relabeled as validation of the new topology. The changed F1/F2 access example, feasible receiving values, mass accounts and through-feeder balancing are checked directly in this revision. There is no simulator, AD/finite-difference, optimization or SUMO experiment in this consolidation. Forward correctness, derivative correctness and external usefulness still require separate evidence.

## Current coverage of the completion standard

| Criterion | Consolidated result | Limit retained |
|---|---|---|
| U1–U2: task/world | Fixed task and representative F1/F2 geometry/permissions; disjoint ownership, route state, eligibility and numerical domain explicit | Numerical geometry/FD calibration and complete scenario-bank values later. |
| U3–U4: evolution/interfaces | Actual-admission allocation, movement-specific residual scan, physical-lane access, elapsed service and endpoint histories form one update | Written realization for review; production implementation and conformance evidence are not supplied. |
| U5: objective | Full source/road population, fixed continuation, clearance domain and sink-exit identity explicit | Numerical horizon qualification and noncompletion handling need later execution. |
| U6: provenance | All current labels and consequential adaptations mapped above | Primary native implementation qualification remains distinct from citation. |
| U7: comparisons | Same physical task, model-owned fine-to-macro conversion, conditioning protocols and common reference mapping/accounting | Exact native B1/B2 adapters, schedules/calibration and program values remain implementation-dependent as agreed. |
| U8: sensitivity | Current local branches, sequential residual effects, timing response and full cost composition given | Numerical derivative correctness, kinks and usefulness remain evidence tasks. |
| U9: joined checks | Explicit scan/access, partial-entry, mass/storage, service and objective hand checks; uniqueness/neutral-domain reasoning | No empirical validation or implementation tests are claimed. |
| U10: explanation | Peer-facing traffic explanations precede every main equation; solver variables and examples are explicit; VSL section order retained | User review of the consolidation is still needed. |

The policy question set is closed. The mathematical transcription and its limits are now reviewable without inventing a waiting-target rule or confusing processing with physical lane access.

